  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 826 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThe broad area of high pressure will flatten during the early to\nmiddle part of next week and potentially begin to retrograde back\nto the west by Wednesday and Thursday. Regardless, the forecast\nwill remain the same for much of the week ahead, very warm and\nhumid with daily chances for diurnally driven convection in the\nafternoon (thanks to the tropical air mass in place underneath\nthis ridge). As a result, heat/humidity and the threat for\nthunderstorms will remain at the forefront of our messaging. Each\nday next week, highs will peak in the upper 80s to lower 90s (with\npeak afternoon heat index values between 100 to 105 degrees) in\nmost locations. NWS Heat Risk highlights a moderate (Level 2/4)\nthreat level in most locations Monday through Thursday, with a few\nof the metro areas (MSL, DCU, HSV) in the Major (Level 3/4) \ncategory.\n\nCoinciding with these heat concerns, the atmosphere will remain \nprimed for low to medium (20-50%) chances for afternoon showers \nand thunderstorms. This activity will be capable of producing \nlocally heavy downpours and potentially localized gusty winds with\nthe strongest activity.",
